---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import MCQuiz from '../../components/MCQuiz.astro';
---

<Layout title='"Turbo Frames vs Turbo Streams?"' description="Practice card. Two Hotwire concepts that get confused. Drills the distinction the interviewer is looking for.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/practice" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Practice
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Interview · Card 5</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">"Turbo Frames vs Turbo Streams?"</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">A favorite for Rails interviews in 2026. They sound similar; they do different things. Pick the answer the interviewer is hoping for.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The question
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">"What's the difference between a Turbo Frame and a Turbo Stream, and when would you use each?"</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <MCQuiz
          options={[
            {
              label: 'A',
              text: 'Turbo Frames are for forms, Turbo Streams are for links.',
              explanation: 'Not how the distinction works. Both can be triggered by forms or links. The difference is who initiates the update.',
            },
            {
              label: 'B',
              text: '<strong>A Turbo Frame</strong> is a region of the page that scopes navigation. Clicks and form submits inside it replace just that region, not the whole page. The client initiates the request; the server responds with HTML for the frame. <strong>A Turbo Stream</strong> is a server-driven update. The server broadcasts an HTML fragment (append, prepend, replace, update, remove) targeting elements by ID, either as part of a form response or over a WebSocket. Use Frames for "click this link, update this part of the page." Use Streams for "show every connected user this new comment as it arrives."',
              explanation: 'Names the difference (client-initiated scope vs server-driven push) and gives a use case for each. The "click this link" vs "push to every user" framing is the cleanest version.',
              correct: true,
            },
            {
              label: 'C',
              text: 'Turbo Frames are server-side; Turbo Streams are client-side.',
              explanation: 'Both are server-rendered HTML. The difference isn\'t where rendering happens — both render on the server.',
            },
            {
              label: 'D',
              text: 'Turbo Frames are an older API; Turbo Streams are the modern replacement.',
              explanation: 'They\'re both current and both useful. Streams didn\'t replace Frames; they cover different use cases.',
            },
          ]}
          principle={`<p>Three sentences capture the senior answer:</p><ol class="list-decimal list-inside ml-4 space-y-1"><li>Frames scope client-initiated navigation to a region of the page.</li><li>Streams push HTML fragments from the server, either as a form response or over WebSocket.</li><li>Pick Frames when the user clicks; pick Streams when something else changes that the user needs to see.</li></ol><p>Bonus: name the Stimulus piece. "For interactivity that doesn\'t need a server round-trip, Stimulus controllers wrap HTML in JS behavior — sortable lists, modal toggles, debounced inputs. It\'s the third Hotwire piece."</p>`}
          theoryHref="/lessons/interview-modern-stack"
          theoryLabel="Interview · The Modern Rails Stack"
        />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/lessons/practice-interview-sidekiq-solid" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Card 4</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-interview-rails-magic" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 6 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
